Yes. In that sense, places and objects can function like addresses for particular configurations of mind.

A gym can cue aggression and effort. A bedroom can cue intimacy. A church can cue reverence. A childhood street can resurrect an older version of you almost instantly. Music, smells, uniforms, tools, even lighting can do the same thing. The “thing” is not merely external; it becomes part of the retrieval path for a state.

That is close to ideas from state-dependent memory, context-dependent memory, affordances, and distributed cognition: cognition is not sealed inside the skull. Environment helps select which patterns become accessible.

So you can think:

place / object / ritual / person → cue → state → perception → behavior

And then the loop reinforces itself. You enter a place expecting a certain state, behave from that state, and the place accumulates more associations with it.

Which means you can also deliberately redesign the map. Make certain rooms, songs, clothes, walks, objects, or routines into reliable entry points for calm, play, focus, tenderness, creativity, courage, whatever you want easier access to.

Not because the object literally contains the state, but because it becomes a handle for retrieving it.

And sometimes leaving a place is powerful for the same reason: you are not only changing scenery—you are stepping outside a network of cues that keeps calling up the same version of you.